    "os.makedirs(WORK_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n",
    "os.chdir(WORK_DIR)\n",
    "\n",
    "DRIVE_DIR = '/content/drive/MyDrive/ParacleaVoice'\n",
    "os.makedirs(DRIVE_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Working directory: {WORK_DIR}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Drive backup:      {DRIVE_DIR}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 1: Install Dependencies\n",
    "\n",
    "**What we install:**\n",
    "- `piper-tts` — the Python library that wraps Piper's ONNX inference and training tools\n",
    "- `onnxruntime` — runs neural networks efficiently on CPU/GPU\n",
    "- `openai-whisper` — OpenAI's speech-to-text model (runs locally, no API key needed)\n",
    "- `ffmpeg` — the Swiss Army knife of audio/video processing\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why these four:** Piper handles the neural TTS. Whisper gives us text labels for each audio clip. ffmpeg slices the MP3 into WAVs. ONNX Runtime executes the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What is TTS?** Text-to-Speech. A neural network reads text and outputs sound waves. Modern TTS (like Piper/VITS) uses a **vocoder** — a network that converts a spectrogram (visual representation of frequencies) into raw audio."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "!pip install -q piper-tts onnxruntime openai-whisper\n",
    "!apt-get update -qq && apt-get install -y -qq ffmpeg\n",
    "\n",
    "# Piper's training code lives in its own repo. We only need the Python src.\n",
    "!git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/rhasspy/piper.git\n",
    "!cd piper/src/python && pip install -q -e .\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n✅ All dependencies installed!\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 2: Get Your Voice Source\n",
    "\n",
    "**The BMO rule:** Use only public-domain audio. No copyright issues, no voice-cloning ethics problems.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Where to find voices:**\n",
    "- [LibriVox](https://librivox.org) — volunteers reading public domain books\n",
    "- [Archive.org](https://archive.org) — old radio dramas, audiobooks\n",
    "- Any book published before 1929 is public domain in the US\n",
    "\n",
    "**What makes a good voice source:**\n",
    "- Single narrator (no dialogue swaps)\n",
    "- Clear speech, minimal music at the start\n",
    "- 20–60 minutes total is plenty for fine-tuning\n",
    "\n",
    "**The code below downloads Alice in Wonderland** (dramatic reading, female narrator, excellent voice acting). Replace the URL with any audiobook you like."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import urllib.request\n",
    "\n",
    "# This is the Alice in Wonderland dramatic reading from LibriVox/Archive.org.\n",
    "# It is 100% public domain. Replace this URL with any audiobook MP3 you want.\n",
    "AUDIO_URL = \"https://archive.org/download/aliceinwonderland_drama_1310_librivo/aliceinwonderland_01_gerstenberg_64kb.mp3\"\n",
    "AUDIO_PATH = f\"{WORK_DIR}/source_audio.mp3\"\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Downloading audiobook...\")\n",
    "urllib.request.urlretrieve(AUDIO_URL, AUDIO_PATH)\n",
    "size_mb = os.path.getsize(AUDIO_PATH) / 1024 / 1024\n",
    "print(f\"✅ Saved: {AUDIO_PATH} ({size_mb:.1f} MB)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 3: Segment the Audio\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this does:** Cuts the long MP3 into many short WAV clips (default: 8 seconds each).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why short clips:** Neural networks learn best from many small examples rather than a few long ones. 8 seconds is the sweet spot — long enough to capture prosody (rhythm, emphasis), short enough that the GPU can process batches efficiently.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Audio format decisions:**\n",
    "| Setting | Value | Reason |\n",
    "|---------|-------|--------|\n",
    "| Sample rate | 22050 Hz | Human speech maxes at ~10 kHz; Nyquist rule says sample >2× |\n",
    "| Channels | 1 (mono) | Voice is single-source; stereo wastes space |\n",
    "| Codec | PCM 16-bit | Uncompressed, lossless, universally supported |\n",
    "\n",
    "**The overlap parameter:** By overlapping clips by 1 second, we ensure no words are cut in half at clip boundaries.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Skip the intro:** The `-skip` parameter (default 180s = 3 min) jumps past opening music/credits."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import subprocess\n",
    "import glob\n",
    "\n",
    "WAV_DIR = f\"{WORK_DIR}/dataset/wavs\"\n",
    "os.makedirs(WAV_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# ffprobe asks ffmpeg how long the file is.\n",
    "result = subprocess.run(\n",
    "    [\"ffprobe\", \"-v\", \"error\", \"-show_entries\", \"format=duration\",\n",
    "     \"-of\", \"default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1\", AUDIO_PATH],\n",
    "    capture_output=True, text=True\n",
    ")\n",
    "duration = float(result.stdout.strip())\n",
    "print(f\"Audio duration: {duration:.1f}s ({duration/60:.1f} min)\")\n",
    "\n",
    "SEGMENT_SEC = 8      # seconds per clip\n",
    "OVERLAP_SEC = 1      # overlap between clips\n",
    "START_SKIP = 180     # skip first 3 minutes (intro)\n",
    "step = SEGMENT_SEC - OVERLAP_SEC\n",
    "\n",
    "idx = 0\n",
    "t = START_SKIP\n",
    "while t + SEGMENT_SEC <= duration:\n",
    "    out_path = f\"{WAV_DIR}/clip_{idx:04d}.wav\"\n",
    "    cmd = [\n",
    "        \"ffmpeg\", \"-y\", \"-i\", AUDIO_PATH,\n",
    "        \"-ss\", str(t), \"-t\", str(SEGMENT_SEC),\n",
    "        \"-ar\", \"22050\", \"-ac\", \"1\", \"-acodec\", \"pcm_s16le\",\n",
    "        out_path\n",
    "    ]\n",
    "    # stdout/stderr suppressed so output stays clean\n",
    "    subprocess.run(cmd, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)\n",
    "    idx += 1\n",
    "    t += step\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"✅ Extracted {idx} segments ({SEGMENT_SEC}s each, {step}s step)\")\n",
    "print(f\"Saved to: {WAV_DIR}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 4: Transcribe with Whisper\n",
    "\n",
    "**What this does:** Runs OpenAI Whisper on every clip to generate a text file saying what was spoken.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why we need transcripts:** A neural TTS model needs **paired data** — audio + the exact text that was said. Without transcripts, the network can't learn the mapping from words to sounds.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How Whisper works (simplified):**\n",
    "1. Convert raw audio into a **spectrogram** (a picture of frequencies over time)\n",
    "2. An **encoder** Transformer reads the spectrogram\n",
    "3. A **decoder** Transformer writes text tokens one at a time\n",
    "4. The result is the transcript\n",
    "\n",
    "**Model choice:** We use `base` (74M parameters) — accurate enough for training data without being slow. `tiny` is faster but makes more errors; errors in training data hurt voice quality.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Multilingual bonus:** Whisper works in 99 languages. Change `language=\"en\"` to `\"ja\"` for Japanese, `\"ko\"` for Korean, etc."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import whisper\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Loading Whisper 'base' model...\")\n",
    "model = whisper.load_model(\"base\")  # 74M params, ~150MB download\n",
    "\n",
    "wav_files = sorted(glob.glob(f\"{WAV_DIR}/*.wav\"))\n",
    "print(f\"Transcribing {len(wav_files)} clips...\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "for i, wav_path in enumerate(wav_files):\n",
    "    txt_path = wav_path.replace(\".wav\", \".txt\")\n",
    "    if os.path.exists(txt_path) and os.path.getsize(txt_path) > 0:\n",
    "        continue  # Skip already-done clips (resumable!)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    result = model.transcribe(wav_path, language=\"en\", fp16=False)\n",
    "    text = result[\"text\"].strip()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    with open(txt_path, \"w\") as f:\n",
    "        f.write(text)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if i % 50 == 0:\n",
    "        print(f\"  [{i}/{len(wav_files)}] {os.path.basename(wav_path)} -> \\\"{text[:50]}...\\\"\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n✅ Transcription complete!\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 5: Build the LJSpeech Dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "**What is LJSpeech format?** The de facto standard for single-speaker TTS datasets. It was created in 2017 for a 24-hour female English dataset and every major TTS tool (Piper, Coqui, etc.) supports it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Structure:**\n",
    "```\n",
    "dataset/\n",
    "  metadata.csv          ← one line per clip\n",
    "  wavs/\n",
    "    clip_0000.wav\n",
    "    clip_0001.wav\n",
    "    ...\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**The CSV format:** `filename|transcript|normalized_transcript`\n",
    "- `clip_0000.wav|Hello world|Hello world`\n",
    "- Column 2 = exactly what was spoken\n",
    "- Column 3 = normalized version (for English, same as column 2)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why this format is genius:** One CSV + one folder. No JSON schemas, no XML, no databases. Any script in any language can parse it in 3 lines."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "entries = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for wav_path in sorted(glob.glob(f\"{WAV_DIR}/*.wav\")):\n",
    "    txt_path = wav_path.replace(\".wav\", \".txt\")\n",
    "    if not os.path.exists(txt_path):\n",
    "        continue\n",
    "    with open(txt_path, \"r\") as f:\n",
    "        transcript = f.read().strip()\n",
    "    if not transcript:\n",
    "        continue\n",
    "    basename = os.path.basename(wav_path)\n",
    "    entries.append(f\"{basename}|{transcript}|{transcript}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "metadata_path = f\"{WORK_DIR}/dataset/metadata.csv\"\n",
    "with open(metadata_path, \"w\") as f:\n",
    "    f.write(\"\\n\".join(entries))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"✅ Dataset ready: {len(entries)} clips\")\n",
    "print(f\"Metadata: {metadata_path}\")\n",
    "print(\"\\nFirst 3 entries:\")\n",
    "for e in entries[:3]:\n",
    "    print(f\"  {e[:100]}...\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 6: Download Base Piper Model\n",
    "\n",
    "**What is a base model?** Think of it as a student who already speaks fluent English. They know:\n",
    "- How to pronounce every phoneme (sounds like `TH`, `SH`, `OW`)\n",
    "- Natural rhythm and intonation (prosody)\n",
    "- How to handle punctuation and numbers\n",
    "\n",
    "**What we teach it:** A new *voice timbre* — the unique sound of the Alice narrator. This is like a voice actor learning to do an impression. Much easier than teaching someone English from scratch.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Piper model sizes:**\n",
    "| Size | Quality | VRAM | Use case |\n",
    "|------|---------|------|----------|\n",
    "| low | Okay | ~1GB | Testing, mobile |\n",
    "| medium | Good | ~2GB | **Best balance — we use this** |\n",
    "| high | Excellent | ~4GB | Desktop/GPU only |\n",
    "\n",
    "We download `en_US-lessac-medium` (female, neutral, clear). You can swap this for any voice from [rhasspy/piper-voices](https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "BASE_MODEL = \"en_US-lessac-medium\"\n",
    "MODELS_DIR = f\"{WORK_DIR}/models\"\n",
    "os.makedirs(MODELS_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# HuggingFace hosts all Piper voices for free direct download.\n",
    "HF_BASE = f\"https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/resolve/main/en/en_US/lessac/medium\"\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Downloading base model...\")\n",
    "!wget -q -O {MODELS_DIR}/{BASE_MODEL}.onnx {HF_BASE}/{BASE_MODEL}.onnx\n",
    "!wget -q -O {MODELS_DIR}/{BASE_MODEL}.onnx.json {HF_BASE}/{BASE_MODEL}.onnx.json\n",
    "\n",
    "onnx_size = os.path.getsize(f\"{MODELS_DIR}/{BASE_MODEL}.onnx\") / 1024 / 1024\n",
    "print(f\"✅ Base model downloaded ({onnx_size:.1f} MB)\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 7: Preprocess for Piper\n",
    "\n",
    "**What preprocessing does:**\n",
    "1. **Phonemization** — converts text to phonetic symbols (`hello` → `HH AH L OW`)\n",
    "2. **Normalization** — lowercases, expands numbers (`42` → `forty-two`), fixes punctuation\n",
    "3. **Feature extraction** — computes mel-spectrograms from WAV files\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why phonemes matter:** English spelling is irregular. "cough", "through", and "bough" all end in `-ough` but sound completely different. Phonemes give the network a consistent, predictable input.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The output:** A cache folder Piper uses during training. You don't interact with it directly."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "!python -m piper_train.preprocess \\\n",
    "    --dataset-dir {WORK_DIR}/dataset \\\n",
    "    --dataset-format ljspeech \\\n",
    "    --sample-rate 22050 \\\n",
    "    --max-workers 4\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n✅ Dataset preprocessed!\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 8: Train the Voice 🚀\n",
    "\n",
    "**What happens during training:**\n",
    "```\n",
    "For each epoch (one full pass through the dataset):\n",
    "  1. Shuffle all clips randomly\n",
    "  2. For each batch of 32 clips:\n",
    "     a. Text → phonemes → neural network → predicted audio\n",
    "     b. Compare predicted audio to real audio (calculate loss)\n",
    "     c. Adjust network weights slightly to reduce loss\n",
    "  3. Every 100 epochs: save a checkpoint to Drive\n",
    "  4. Every epoch: measure validation loss (how well it generalizes)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "**Hyperparameters explained:**\n",
    "| Parameter | Value | What it controls |\n",
    "|-----------|-------|------------------|\n",
    "| `max-epochs` | 5000 | How many times to see the full dataset |\n",
    "| `batch-size` | 32 | Clips processed in parallel (higher = faster, needs more VRAM) |\n",
    "| `learning-rate` | 0.0001 | How aggressively weights change (lower = slower but stabler) |\n",
    "| `validation-split` | 0.05 | 5% of data held back to test generalization |\n",
    "| `checkpoint-epochs` | 100 | Save a snapshot every 100 epochs |\n",
    "\n",
    "**How long:** ~4–6 hours on a free T4 GPU. You can stop and resume anytime — checkpoints are saved to your Drive.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What to watch for:** Validation loss should steadily decrease. If it starts rising, the model is **overfitting** (memorizing instead of learning). Stop early if that happens."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import shutil\n",
    "\n",
    "CHECKPOINT_DIR = f\"{DRIVE_DIR}/checkpoints\"\n",
    "os.makedirs(CHECKPOINT_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "EPOCHS = 5000\n",
    "BATCH_SIZE = 32\n",
    "LEARNING_RATE = 0.0001\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Starting training for {EPOCHS} epochs...\")\n",
    "print(f\"Checkpoints saved to: {CHECKPOINT_DIR}\")\n",
    "print(f\"\\n⏱️  Estimated time: {EPOCHS // 1000 + 4} hours on T4 GPU\")\n",
    "print(\"💡 If Colab disconnects, re-run all cells. Training resumes from the latest checkpoint.\\n\")\n",
    "\n",
    "!python -m piper_train \\\n",
    "    --dataset-dir {WORK_DIR}/dataset \\\n",
    "    --accelerator gpu \\\n",
    "    --batch-size {BATCH_SIZE} \\\n",
    "    --validation-split 0.05 \\\n",
    "    --num-test-examples 5 \\\n",
    "    --max-epochs {EPOCHS} \\\n",
    "    --checkpoint-epochs 100 \\\n",
    "    --checkpoint-dir {CHECKPOINT_DIR} \\\n",
    "    --optimizer adamw \\\n",
    "    --lr {LEARNING_RATE}\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\n✅ Training complete!\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 9: Export to ONNX\n",
    "\n",
    "**What is ONNX?** Open Neural Network Exchange — a universal format for ML models. An ONNX model runs on:\n",
    "- CPU (via ONNX Runtime)\n",
    "- NVIDIA GPU (CUDA)\n",
    "- AMD GPU (ROCm)\n",
    "- Apple Silicon (CoreML)\n",
    "- Even web browsers (ONNX.js)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why export:** During training, Piper uses PyTorch checkpoints (`.ckpt`). These are huge and tied to Python. ONNX is a compact, language-agnostic format your Rust `piper-tts-rs` crate can load directly.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The config JSON:** Contains metadata like sample rate, speaker count, and phoneme set. We copy it from the base model and rename it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import glob\n",
    "\n",
    "ckpts = sorted(glob.glob(f\"{CHECKPOINT_DIR}/*.ckpt\"))\n",
    "if not ckpts:\n",
    "    print(\"ERROR: No checkpoints found!\")\n",
    "else:\n",
    "    best_ckpt = ckpts[-1]\n",
    "    print(f\"Using checkpoint: {os.path.basename(best_ckpt)}\")\n",
    "    \n",
    "    OUTPUT_ONNX = f\"{DRIVE_DIR}/paraclea.onnx\"\n",
    "    \n",
    "    !python -m piper_train.export_onnx \\\n",
    "        --checkpoint {best_ckpt} \\\n",
    "        --output {OUTPUT_ONNX}\n",
    "    \n",
    "    shutil.copy(f\"{MODELS_DIR}/{BASE_MODEL}.onnx.json\", f\"{DRIVE_DIR}/paraclea.onnx.json\")\n",
    "    \n",
    "    size_mb = os.path.getsize(OUTPUT_ONNX) / 1024 / 1024\n",
    "    print(f\"\\n✅ Voice model exported!\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  ONNX: {OUTPUT_ONNX} ({size_mb:.1f} MB)\")\n",
    "    print(f\"  JSON: {DRIVE_DIR}/paraclea.onnx.json\")"
   ]
  },
  {
